findings.body:
`relaunchWindow` saves the climbed route, then calls `kickoff`, and only after that writes the `respawn` event:

```ts
recordSessionRoute(db, sessionId, route);
...
db.prepare('UPDATE sessions SET tmux_target = ? WHERE id = ?').run(pane.paneId, sessionId);
const delivered = kickoff(pane, contextMarkdown + extras.promptSuffix);
appendEvent(db, sessionId, 'respawn', { delivered, ...extras.eventPayload });
```

`kickoff` (`src/claude/session-runtime.service.ts` L886-890) calls `steerPane` without catching. `steerPane` throws `SteerNotDeliveredError` when the input box is absent, can't be cleared, or the paste never lands. When that happens, the row already holds rung+1 and no `respawn` event exists. `windowFailure` (`routing.utils.ts` L172-183) only clears a failure at a `respawn` event, so the failure that caused this climb is still counted.

The CLI's error message says "Re-run `pup respawn <session>`" (`src/cli/index.ts` L1773-1776). `isHandoffReady` is still true, so the retry goes straight to `respawnRoute`, starts from the stored rung+1, sees the same failure, and climbs to rung+2. On the two-rung critical ladder, one rejection plus one failed paste ends in `TopRungReachedError`. `pup kill --respawn` shares `relaunchWindow` and has the same problem. Decision 92 says one failure climbs one rung once.

The new window is open either way, so the `respawn` event should be written whether or not the paste lands.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/8d1ee84cc1beb600120f41a51e4527188dc2319e/src/core/session-handoff.service.ts#L303-L309

findings.suggestion:
let delivered = false;
try {
  delivered = kickoff(pane, contextMarkdown + extras.promptSuffix);
} finally {
  appendEvent(db, sessionId, 'respawn', { delivered, ...extras.eventPayload });
}

findings.body:
Decision 92 says an explicit `--model`/`--effort` "lifts the top-rung refusal, since the operator has decided". The code can't tell an operator override from a conductor override. `pup launch` only refuses `callingSession(db)`, never `callingConductor()`:

```ts
if (callingSession(db)) {
  return refuse('`pup launch` is operator-only; sessions cannot launch sessions.');
}
```

In `session-lifecycle.service.ts` L220-222, `isOverride` is true whenever either flag is set, regardless of who passed it, and `if (climb.topReached && !isOverride) throw new TopRungReachedError(...)`.

With `pup conductor start --worker-model X`, the kickoff tells the conductor to run `` `pup launch <task> --model X` `` for every launch (`profile-compiler.service.ts` L571, L591, L609). So every conductor relaunch is an override, the top-rung refusal never fires, and the Models section's "A launch refused at the top rung… report it, never retry it" can't trigger. A task that keeps stalling or dying on Opus xhigh is relaunched on the top rung instead of reaching the operator, and the new session replaces the killed one, so the `top-rung` need disappears from Needs you. Gate rejections still end in `blocked`, so the loop is limited to stall and dead-turn climbs.

The `--worker-model` → `--model` pass-through predates this PR. The top-rung refusal it now bypasses is new. Decision 92 doesn't address conductor-issued overrides. Consider refusing an override that lifts a top-rung refusal when `callingConductor()` is true, or passing `--worker-model` through a channel that doesn't count as an override at the top rung.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/8d1ee84cc1beb600120f41a51e4527188dc2319e/src/cli/index.ts#L976-L1016
